WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.569 --> 00:00:07.640
Antoine Allen: How are you doing guys?

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:15.759
Today we have very special guest Ms. Emelyn
Stuart of Stuart Cinema Cafe and founder of

00:00:15.759 --> 00:00:17.430
the October Film Festival.

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:19.110
How are you doing today?

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:20.780
Evelyn Stuart: I’m doing wonderful.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:21.790
How are you?

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:22.790
Yes, yes.

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:23.790
Thank you!

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:25.720
Antoine: You are actually our first guest.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:26.720
Evelyn: What?

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:27.720
Antoine: Yes.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.140
We’ve got this amazing platform on Film
Courage.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:31.140
Evelyn: Wonderful.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:32.290
Shout out to Film Courage.

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:36.120
Antoine: And we decided to have you as the
first.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:37.130
Evelyn: I feel so honored.

00:00:37.130 --> 00:00:41.610
Antoine: Yes, because the title of this series
is Put Your Money Where Your Mouth Is.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:42.610
Evelyn: Whew!

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:43.610
All my money!

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:47.140
All the kids money, all the college fund,
I put everything, I went all in.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:48.140
Lester Greene: She went all in.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:53.219
Antoine: And this is an important series because
I believe that people want people to invest

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:54.219
in them.

00:00:54.219 --> 00:00:55.980
And they have to realize you have to invest
in yourself.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:56.980
Evelyn: Yes!

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:57.980
Antoine: So tell me your journey.

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:00.739
What made you get into this crazy world of
entertainment?

00:01:00.739 --> 00:01:02.340
Evelyn: Wow!

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:05.540
That’s a loaded question?

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:07.140
Yeah, right!

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:10.880
So I have a real estate background.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:14.280
I’m really a business person who happens
to be in the arts.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.960
Somebody gave me a script, I read it.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:20.430
I thought “Oohh, I want to help this guy
tell his story.”

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:23.119
I called him up and said “What do you need?”

00:01:23.119 --> 00:01:24.119
He said “Everything.”

00:01:24.119 --> 00:01:25.170
I had no idea what everything was.

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.229
Everything was a lot of money.

00:01:27.229 --> 00:01:31.270
And then I produced it and it won some awards
and I thought “Wow?

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:34.369
If I can figure out how to monetize this,
I could do this for a lot of people.”

00:01:34.369 --> 00:01:37.310
Antoine: So you had the passion of giving
early on.

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:40.740
Emelyn: Yes, I couldn’t have said that better
myself.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:47.470
Antoine: So you started with a filmmaker that
needed help, it won awards because of you.

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:49.320
Do you still keep in contact?

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:50.320
Emelyn: Yes.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.750
Antoine: What’s his name, we’ll give him
a shout out?

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:59.140
I guess not…

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:06.030
So I want to know now is you did that, what
made you want to start a festival (the October

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:07.030
Film Festival)?

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:12.690
Emelyn: Yes so I went to a lot of film festivals
as you guys have and there was just stuff

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:13.690
that was missing.

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:16.519
I felt like a lot of festivals (no offense
to anyone out there.

00:02:16.519 --> 00:02:17.850
I love all you guys).

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:23.299
But some of the festivals were very political
and I felt like I wanted to start a festival

00:02:23.299 --> 00:02:24.500
that was clean.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:28.010
And by that I mean where it doesn’t matter
who is in your film.

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:30.150
It doesn’t matter how many followers you
have.

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:37.040
It’s strictly based on you being a filmmaker
who can tell a compelling story.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:38.040
And so I said You know what?

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:41.579
I’m going to start my own film festival
where we could do that.

00:02:41.579 --> 00:02:47.519
Where we could just give a platform and celebrate
filmmakers for being filmmakers.

00:02:47.519 --> 00:02:52.519
Antoine: Yes, that’s great because a lot
of filmmakers out there (if you don’t know

00:02:52.519 --> 00:02:55.290
this), a lot of those turn into a business.

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:59.600
They are showing three hundred films, taking
submission fees and half the time they barely

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:00.600
see you.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:04.280
So one thing I like about the October Film
Festival is it’s very intimate and very

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:05.280
engaging.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:06.860
Evelyn: We’re a teaching festival.

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:08.199
Antoine: …A teaching festival.

00:03:08.199 --> 00:03:12.270
When you started the festival did you even
realize how many lives you had touched?

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:13.600
Emelyn: I had no idea.

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:18.880
I still can’t really wrap my head around
all of the people that I see on television

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:22.709
now, the awards…I mean even you guys.

00:03:22.709 --> 00:03:25.050
Just to see all the stuff that you’ve done
and that I know you.

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:26.990
Sometimes I see you and I’m like “Ahh!

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:28.460
Look at them.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:29.460
How wonderful.”

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:32.270
Antoine: And to my viewers, that’s how we
met.

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:35.989
I was a regular filmmaker, I submitted my
short film LIFE IS TOO SHORT…

00:03:35.989 --> 00:03:37.650
Evelyn: Actually somebody told me about you.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:40.959
Antoine: Yes somebody told you…and this
is all about putting your money where your

00:03:40.959 --> 00:03:41.979
mouth is.

00:03:41.979 --> 00:03:49.570
So a little background story is Emelyn’s
festival was at this poet day in Harlem.

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:54.290
I actually rented it out a few months before
and I sold it out (packed the house).

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:55.290
Emelyn: Multiple times.

00:03:55.290 --> 00:04:00.100
Antoine: Multiple times and my name started
buzzing and she started hearing my name and

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:03.100
I got to the festival and it felt like home.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:06.590
And at the big festivals you can get lost
because if you don’t have a big name for

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:13.810
your festival or a big publicist you literally
get lost, you never know about networking

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:14.810
and contacting people.

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:17.709
Since then we’ve been doing business and
in partnerships.

00:04:17.709 --> 00:04:21.720
Emelyn: And that also speaks to you never
know who is watching.

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:27.540
Because then I looked you up and I was just
kind of following and watching before I said

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:32.870
not only do I want your film in the festival,
but I also want you to come and speak to filmmakers

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:34.850
about how you did this thing.

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:39.240
How did you sell out a theater multiple times
and then I’m thinking because I had never

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:40.240
heard of you…so how did he do that?

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:45.980
So I thought if he could do it, other people
can do it, we just don’t know how.

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:46.980
And that’s why we’re a teaching festival.

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:52.150
And I said “You know, we need to come and
have him talk to filmmakers about how he did

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:53.150
this thing.”

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:56.710
Antoine: That’s a lesson for filmmakers
out there, put your best foot forward all

00:04:56.710 --> 00:04:59.270
the time because you never know who is watching.

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:05.180
So now you’re this business woman, you have
this festival, you help filmmakers, now you

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:07.500
decided…start your own theater.

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:12.800
Now what’s going on in that head why you
want to do this now?

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:17.920
Emelyn: I think that a lot of what I do, it’s
an answer to my own problem.

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:24.070
So I was renting space every year for the
festival and I felt like the venues were treating

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:30.400
us badly, considering we were spending $12,000
to $14,000 for four days to rent a space,

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:35.640
I felt like the venues did not understand
filmmakers and the importance of the Q&amp;As

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:40.260
(some of them were rushed) and there was a
year when they turned the lights off on us

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:41.260
during our red carpet.

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:42.350
Antoine: I was there for that year.

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:44.780
Emelyn: Yes, he was there for that.

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:45.780
And it was just devastating.

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:50.880
Or when a movie doesn’t play and they’re
like “We’ve got to keep moving to too

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:51.880
bad.”

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:54.480
And I thought this can’t continue to happen.

00:05:54.480 --> 00:06:00.360
I want to be able to control when we have
the festival, when the filmmakers have events,

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:04.830
they should be treated better and I think
what better way to do that than to start my

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:09.250
own theater where I can give distribution
in terms of theater runs, where I can help

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:15.570
filmmakers make money and monetize their talent,
grow their audience and then at the same time

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:19.990
not have to deal with venues who don’t understand
what we do.

00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:24.300
When you go into a place where there is a
four wall you don’t necessarily understand

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:28.720
all of the issues that filmmakers have when
they are trying to show their film.

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:31.230
Lester: We are here at Stuart Cinema Cafe.

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:36.670
Antoine: So we’re here at this famous movie
theater that you started from the ground up,

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:38.630
tell us what are the services?

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:40.320
What do you provide?

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:45.140
Emelyn: You know it’s interesting you called
it a famous theater.

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:48.860
And I heard someone earlier this week refer
to it in the same way.

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:51.650
And I said “Why do you call it famous?”

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:57.760
And she said “Because you are the first
Black Latina to own a movie theater.

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:03.930
And have a motion picture license and all
the stuff that comes with that.

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:05.130
And I thought “Wow?

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:08.470
Some people think this is a historical first…

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:10.320
Lester: A movement.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:12.169
Emelyn: Yes…a movement.

00:07:12.169 --> 00:07:18.620
Antoine: And that is one of the main reasons
is we have so many theaters out there and

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:22.130
you don’t know what the owners look like.

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:26.100
This is a strong connection because there
is nothing like seeing your own self, your

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:27.500
own people and think “Wow!

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:28.980
I could do that.”

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:37.340
And I want to segue to my next question…there
are young females out there that want to direct,

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:38.680
that want to produce.

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:46.400
How do you balance being a woman in this man-dominated
industry?

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:49.580
Emelyn: Ah…yes.

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:55.240
I will say I had major challenges building
this place.

00:07:55.240 --> 00:08:02.120
All the contractors and business people that
I was dealing with, they really had a hard

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:04.630
time listening to what I was saying.

00:08:04.630 --> 00:08:11.060
I’m a producer so I’m always looking for
money and there is this idea that we women

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:13.850
do not know how to manage money, that we’re
not a good investment.

00:08:13.850 --> 00:08:15.419
Antoine: In 2018.

00:08:15.419 --> 00:08:18.030
Emelyn: And it’s changing, right?

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:19.080
It’s changing.

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:23.560
But that was also one of the struggles I had
when I was doing this because people really

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:27.960
that like I didn’t know what I was talking
about.

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:35.020
So I think part of it is, you’ve got to
have a lot of your own skin in the game especially

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:36.289
as a women, right?

00:08:36.289 --> 00:08:44.130
Because if you are going to wait for somebody
to write you a check based on your talents

00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:45.510
and your visions, that may not happen.

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:48.529
So you’ve got to figure out how to make
it happen for yourself.

00:08:48.529 --> 00:08:53.380
I don’t need a man to validate me.

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:58.890
And I feel like we all have our different
strengths and weaknesses but I know what I

00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:00.089
bring to the table.

00:09:00.089 --> 00:09:04.029
I think that’s critical, you have to know
what you bring to the table.

00:09:04.029 --> 00:09:09.290
If you’re coming in with your two arms swinging,
you can’t eat at that table.

00:09:09.290 --> 00:09:14.350
So if you know what you bring to the table,
you have a much better chance of making it.

00:09:14.350 --> 00:09:18.339
Antoine: And to the filmmakers out there,
you have to bring value.

00:09:18.339 --> 00:09:20.660
Your talent is just not enough.

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:23.110
If you have talent, that’s like a bonus.

00:09:23.110 --> 00:09:27.790
So if there is a table, you better bring the
knife, you better bring the meat, you better

00:09:27.790 --> 00:09:31.880
bring the milk because we are in an industry
now that it just doesn’t matter how talented

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:32.880
you are.

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:37.270
Emelyn: I almost think it’s not about talent,
as much.

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:44.470
I mean it matters but if you take two actors
who are equally good, it’s the one with

00:09:44.470 --> 00:09:46.890
the hustle that gets the audition.

00:09:46.890 --> 00:09:53.580
It’s the one that is sending out the headshots,
that’s walking around the streets looking

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:54.580
for work.

00:09:54.580 --> 00:09:56.410
That’s the person getting the job.

00:09:56.410 --> 00:10:01.210
And it doesn’t mean that they are more talented,
it means they’ve got that drive.

00:10:01.210 --> 00:10:05.820
Antoine: And that’s why this series is called
put your money where your mouth is because

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:07.290
the hustle is sold separate.

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:11.390
I’m a filmmaker, Lester is an actor, we’ve
both won multiple awards.

00:10:11.390 --> 00:10:15.230
There are people out there that are better
than us, but what happens is we are out hustling

00:10:15.230 --> 00:10:16.230
everybody.

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:17.230
Lester: I don’t know about that.

00:10:17.230 --> 00:10:18.230
There is no one better than me.

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:21.010
But I work harder than them.

00:10:21.010 --> 00:10:25.010
Antoine: So you work harder and that’s what
you’ve got to do now.

00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:28.460
You go into meetings and they ask you how
many Instagram followers you have.

00:10:28.460 --> 00:10:30.100
You’re like what?

00:10:30.100 --> 00:10:31.880
It becomes a numbers game.

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:37.560
Also being a founder/owner, how does it feel
having kids?

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:38.850
What’s that balance like?

00:10:38.850 --> 00:10:40.520
Emelyn: Wow, that’s tough.

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:45.690
I have also said the hardest part of being
in the business is how many times I have to

00:10:45.690 --> 00:10:47.750
say goodbye to my children.

00:10:47.750 --> 00:10:51.790
Just in the morning, and I travel a lot and
I’m gone a lot and it’s hard.

00:10:51.790 --> 00:10:57.470
But I’ll say this I live in every moment
that I’m in separately.

00:10:57.470 --> 00:11:01.310
So tonight I’m here with you, I’m in this
moment.

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:03.220
I’m celebrating what’s happening here.

00:11:03.220 --> 00:11:07.040
When I go home I’m in that moment with my
children.

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:10.870
I am the mommy, I am the homework, I am doing
all that stuff.

00:11:10.870 --> 00:11:13.060
Lester: Oh…so you don’t take your work
home?

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:15.589
Emelyn: It’s one moment at a time.

00:11:15.589 --> 00:11:18.540
When I’m with my mom and we’re having
coffee, I’m a daughter and at that moment,

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:19.540
that’s what I do.

00:11:19.540 --> 00:11:25.220
Antoine: And that’s the beautiful thing,
you have to learn to be different people.

00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:27.910
If you’re married, you’ve got to be a
husband or wife that night.

00:11:27.910 --> 00:11:29.990
If you own a company, you have to be a CEO
that night.

00:11:29.990 --> 00:11:33.529
So you have to wear multiple hats and sometimes
it confusing.

00:11:33.529 --> 00:11:36.640
But what do you do to detox?

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:39.010
Having all this with the detox moments?

00:11:39.010 --> 00:11:45.810
Emelyn: I read a lot and believe it or not,
I love reading about the industry.

00:11:45.810 --> 00:11:51.010
So a lot of times you see me posting stuff
that I learned.

00:11:51.010 --> 00:11:53.130
I am hungry for knowledge.

00:11:53.130 --> 00:12:01.300
And the more that I learn, the more happier
and fulfilled I feel because I think that

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:03.410
there are just some rooms that we’re not
going to get invited to.

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:04.780
Lester: But I love that you share that knowledge.

00:12:04.780 --> 00:12:05.780
Emelyn: Absolutely.

00:12:05.780 --> 00:12:08.980
Lester: Some people are like I’m going to
hold onto it and I don’t want anybody else

00:12:08.980 --> 00:12:10.070
to get ahead.

00:12:10.070 --> 00:12:13.920
You can’t get ahead that way.

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:14.920
Emelyn: You can’t.

00:12:14.920 --> 00:12:15.920
Antoine: And Lester, you’re an actor.

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:16.920
You’re in the business.

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:17.920
What do you do to detox?

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:23.760
Lester: [Laughs] Well, usually what I like
to do is just stay home, hang out with my

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:27.140
partner, hang out with my dog.

00:12:27.140 --> 00:12:31.690
Sometimes you have to actually get away from
what you do in order to actually enjoy it.

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:32.690
Antoine: Disconnect.

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:37.050
Lester: It’s almost like you shut down and
then you reboot.

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:38.050
Emelyn: That’s true.

00:12:38.050 --> 00:12:39.100
Lester: And then you go back into that world.

00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:40.300
You have to do that.

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:41.500
Because you’ll drive yourself crazy.

00:12:41.500 --> 00:12:43.190
Antoine: Yes, I think everybody needs that.

00:12:43.190 --> 00:12:47.890
I travel and get away from everything and
then I come back refreshed.

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:51.490
Emelyn: I text when you’re traveling about
work all the time.

00:12:51.490 --> 00:12:54.190
Antoine: Well that’s the thing, everybody
has their priorities.

00:12:54.190 --> 00:12:56.880
Emelyn: He was in the middle of…where were
you?

00:12:56.880 --> 00:12:57.880
Antoine: In Dubai.

00:12:57.880 --> 00:12:58.880
Emelyn: Were you?

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:04.980
I’m like texting, asking questions because
we do a lot of business together and then

00:13:04.980 --> 00:13:05.980
I thought “Wait?

00:13:05.980 --> 00:13:07.110
He’s in Dubai.

00:13:07.110 --> 00:13:08.800
I shouldn’t be texting.”

00:13:08.800 --> 00:13:10.720
Antoine: That’s called a priority list.

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:14.339
In this industry there are people on the priority
list that you’re going to get right back

00:13:14.339 --> 00:13:15.339
to.

00:13:15.339 --> 00:13:17.990
There are going to be people where you see
the text but you’re not going to get right

00:13:17.990 --> 00:13:19.250
back to because they are not on that priority
list.

00:13:19.250 --> 00:13:20.250
Emelyn: Oh…that’s terrible.

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:21.250
That’s bad.

00:13:21.250 --> 00:13:23.320
I hope on on the top of that list everywhere.

00:13:23.320 --> 00:13:24.610
Antione: It’s true.

00:13:24.610 --> 00:13:25.899
Lester: You are.

00:13:25.899 --> 00:13:27.620
You’re on our show.

00:13:27.620 --> 00:13:32.770
Emelyn: So now someone who text me today and
who didn’t hear back from me and watches

00:13:32.770 --> 00:13:34.810
this is going to be like…

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:37.530
Lester: Oh, Emelyn doesn’t think I’m top
priority.

00:13:37.530 --> 00:13:39.570
Antoine: Listen guys, it’s not fair.

00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:41.960
This is what it is, it happens.

00:13:41.960 --> 00:13:47.839
Another question I wanted to ask you, doing
all this stuff, doing this business, you are

00:13:47.839 --> 00:13:50.649
setting yourself up for your legacy.

00:13:50.649 --> 00:13:55.170
When you are not hear anymore, what is one
thing you want people to say about you?

00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:57.460
Emelyn: She cared.

00:13:57.460 --> 00:13:59.750
Because I care.

00:13:59.750 --> 00:14:02.800
I really, really, care.

00:14:02.800 --> 00:14:04.160
Antoine: What about you Lester?

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:06.560
Lester: Well…I care about everybody.

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:08.420
I’m always looking at…

00:14:08.420 --> 00:14:12.250
Emelyn: That’s my line!

00:14:12.250 --> 00:14:19.910
You cannot say…you didn’t care five minutes
ago.

00:14:19.910 --> 00:14:25.140
Lester: I just want people to realize that
I made a different.

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:27.600
I want people to say “Wow, Lester was a
great guy.

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:28.730
He helped me out.

00:14:28.730 --> 00:14:32.350
I remember when I needed an agent and he got
me that agent.

00:14:32.350 --> 00:14:35.180
I remember when he gave me advice on how to
start my own film.

00:14:35.180 --> 00:14:36.680
Emelyn: And he wrote that great book.

00:14:36.680 --> 00:14:37.680
Lester: Right, exactly.

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:40.070
You can get that on Amazon (‘Act Like You
Love Yourself’).

00:14:40.070 --> 00:14:43.019
That’s why I did the book, because I wanted
to give back to the people.

00:14:43.019 --> 00:14:44.980
I want people to get something.

00:14:44.980 --> 00:14:48.090
I don’t want to feel like I am taking everything
for myself.

00:14:48.090 --> 00:14:49.230
It’s not about that.

00:14:49.230 --> 00:14:50.480
Antoine: Yeah, guys.

00:14:50.480 --> 00:14:52.510
This was UNSCRIPTED.

00:14:52.510 --> 00:14:54.150
Lester: …Conversations.

00:14:54.150 --> 00:14:55.790
Emelyn: Unscripted…yes!

00:14:55.790 --> 00:15:00.130
Lester: All this stuff that we talked about,
there was definitely no script.

00:15:00.130 --> 00:15:01.860
Emelyn: There was definitely no script.

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:03.500
Antoine: So guys this is UNSCRIPTED.

00:15:03.500 --> 00:15:04.510
Lester: Conversations.

00:15:04.510 --> 00:15:05.560
Antoine: Antoine Allen.

00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:06.560
Lester: Lester Greene.

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:07.640
Emelyn: Emelyn Stuart.

00:15:07.640 --> 00:15:09.560
Antoine: Film Courage.

